-- Insert updated INSIDE THE ROW VOL. 2
INSERT INTO newsletters (title, quarter, year, content, is_published, published_date)
VALUES (
  'INSIDE THE ROW — VOL. 2',
  'VOL. 2',
  2025,
  E'What We''re Building at Founders Row\n\nOver the last four months, as we''ve launched Founders Row, three things have become extremely apparent to me.\n\nFirst, there is a massive hole in the market when it comes to advising, helping, and partnering with early-stage founders. What exists today is broken. On one end, you have private equity firms that are often predatory at this stage — trying to take control far too early. On the other end, you have consultants with no real skin in the game, collecting monthly or annual retainers with incentives that have nothing to do with outcomes.\n\nFor early-stage founders trying to figure out how to take the next step — how to grow, how to professionalize, how to scale without losing their vision — it''s a no-win situation. That''s the first and most glaring gap I see.\n\nThe second thing that has surprised me is how willing founders are to give up equity simply for the right partnership. Many don''t need capital. Some need a little, some need none at all. What they really need is guidance — someone who''s been there, someone aligned long term, and someone who will help them think clearly about what comes next. As we all know, nothing replaces experience — and we have a lot of it.\n\nThe third thing, which is maybe the most surprising, is that no one has built a firm around this opportunity. Everywhere we look, we see small private equity firms, small consulting shops, and a few large venture studios that mostly live in tech. But there''s no one really playing in the consumer space — especially multi-unit experiential brands — and no one truly supporting early-stage DTC founders like Hotbox Pretzels, Kühler Technologies, or Yaitte, a women''s clothing brand out of London that we''ve been talking to for the last month and a half. Incredible product, great numbers — they just need guidance.\n\nThat''s the backdrop for Founders Row.\n\n\nThe Model Is Becoming Very Clear\n\nThe path for Founders Row has become very clear, very quickly. We do two things.\n\n\n1. Incubation — Building from the Ground Up\n\nWe incubate brands either on our own or alongside founders.\n\nIn the next 60–90 days, we plan to raise capital for two incubated brands.\n\nThe first is The Baking Social, in partnership with Shane Quillen, founder of Sugar Shane''s. We love this concept and see a real opportunity to build something special.\n\nHow This Builds Long-Term Value\n\nI also want to be clear about how this builds long-term value for all of us — the owners of Founders Row.\n\nLet''s use The Baking Social as an example.\n\nWe plan to raise $1.5 million at a $7.5 million valuation, selling 20% non-dilutive equity. Minimum checks will be $100,000 (roughly 1.33%). That capital will be used to build two to three proof-of-concept studios. If those do well, we''ll raise a Series B based on EBITDA, expand to 12–20 locations, and then sell the business in under five years — most likely closer to 36 months.\n\n• Founders Row receives 10% of the raise, or $150,000\n• Shane Quillen and Founders Row retain 80% ownership, split 51% Founders Row / 29% Shane\n• After year one, Founders Row begins receiving a $100,000 annual platform fee, increasing to $200,000 in year three, and continuing until exit\n\nSo from one incubated brand, Founders Row has:\n\n• a raise fee (10%)\n• recurring platform fees ($100K–$300K annually over time)\n• and 51% ownership of the business\n(an exit at $50M would return ~$25M to Founders Row)\n\nThe second incubated brand is Dream Digital Meditation.\n\nThis one has hit a few speed bumps — not roadblocks — just the reality of how fast this space is evolving. The technology around digital meditation and digital psychedelics is changing faster than any consumer wellness category I''ve ever seen. We took a step back with Reality Center in Santa Monica to make sure they were the right partner, and what we realized over the last 30 days is simple: this is a space we want to play in, but if you know me, I like to measure twice and cut once.\n\nWe''ll raise for Dream sometime in February or March, once we''re certain we''ve got it right — because we plan to be the first to truly take this to market. We''ve already been in touch with two major family offices asking for a first look at the $3M raise, so we''ll see where that goes.\n\n\n2. Platform Partnerships — Long-Term Alignment\n\nThe second part of Founders Row is being a platform partner.\n\nA great example of this is SculptHouse here in Atlanta. SculptHouse has two locations — Atlanta and Dallas — and has been operating for ten years. The founder tried to scale and franchise early in the pandemic and had to close a few studios, but the two that survived are thriving.\n\nThose two locations generate about $1M of four-wall EBITDA and roughly $650,000 of net income after HQ costs. She''s ready to scale, and we''re ready to help.\n\nInstead of the original plan — invest $1.5M and take 40% — we structured something far more founder-friendly, cash-flow accretive to Founders Row, with an outcome that pays us like a 20% equity owner on exit.\n\nFounders Row will invest $350,000 of GP capital to build the third studio in Midtown Atlanta. In return, we receive:\n\n• a $200,000 annual platform fee on a five-year contract\n• stepping up to $300,000 annually once the fifth studio opens (within ~24 months)\n• plus a 20% success fee on any recap or sale, with protections around debt\n\nThe founder maintains 100% equity control. We then help scale the business from three studios to ten using a mix of cash flow, modest debt, or a small raise that doesn''t change our economics.\n\nThis is one of the most founder-friendly structures I''ve ever seen — and one I wish existed eight years ago when I took Honors Holdings to market. Ask yourself how many founders would do deals like this with Jamie and the Founders Row team. We think the number is large — which means we need to be selective — but the ocean is very big.\n\n\nAdditional Platform Examples\n\nThis same model applies to Hotbox Pretzels, which you''ll see a press release about this week. Hotbox is led by Roman Desmond, a 23-year-old founder I''ve known for years. We''ve signed an exclusive agreement where Founders Row receives 20% of the Class A equity in a non-dilutive structure in exchange for advisory and consulting services.\n\nHotbox is doing just shy of $1M in DTC revenue with roughly $100,000 of net profit annually. We''re cleaning up marketing, working through manufacturing partnerships, and are confident this business could sell for $5–15M in the next 12–24 months. At a $10M sale with no debt, that''s a $2M outcome for Founders Row, with zero capital invested.\n\nAnother similar example is Kühler Technologies, which has developed a hand-cooling device that improves athletic performance by lowering core body temperature. We''re finalizing a deal similar to Hotbox, where Founders Row will receive 5–10% of any exit or recap, with platform fees introduced over time as the business grows.\n\nAll of these partnerships have happened with essentially zero advertising — just one press release in the first four months. That will change in early 2026, as the Founders Row marketing and press machine starts its engine.\n\n\nWhere We''re Headed\n\nOur goal is to incubate one to two brands per year and be a platform partner to three to seven brands per year.\n\nAs we continue to build out the team over the next 90 days, you''ll see more press around Founders Row and a clearer articulation of where we''re going. Over the next five years, there''s a very high likelihood we will have incubated 10+ brands and partnered with 40+ founders, owning meaningful equity in some and earning recurring platform fees and exit participation across many.\n\nOne ask of all of you: introductions to high-net-worth individuals and family offices are welcomed. These deals are intentionally structured to be founder-friendly, investor-friendly, and aligned with Founders Row. I know it sounds almost impossible to build a model where the founder wins, the platform wins, and the investor wins — but it''s going to be my life''s mission to prove that it''s possible.\n\nThere''s a reason I asked you to join the GP. It wasn''t because of a lack of capital. It''s because I like to win with family and friends. I''ve won alone, and it was the loneliest thing I''ve ever done. When I''ve won with others, it''s been some of the most fulfilling moments of my life.\n\n\nA Personal Note\n\nOn a more personal note, December has been a really good month for our family. Maggie is home from college, and for the first time in a while, the four of us are all back together again. It feels like things are right in the world.\n\nHaving your oldest go to college — which many of you have experienced — has been harder than I expected. Not just for Ragan and me, but for Charley as well. We''re a very close family, and having the four of us together matters more than I probably ever realized before. It makes the holidays that much more joyful.\n\nThe day after Christmas, we''ll head back down to Baker''s Bay for about a week and a half. The girls may stay a little longer. I''ll come home around January 3rd or 4th and get back to work.\n\n2025 started as a tumultuous year with my exit and departure from Sweathouz — a brand I loved and built from day one. But as the year comes to a close, it''s become very clear to me that this has been, by far, the greatest year of my life.\n\nA big part of that is clarity. I feel like I''m truly on a mission now — to fix the relationship between founders, private capital, and growth. To build something that allows founders to stay whole, businesses to scale responsibly, and capital to be aligned the right way.\n\nThat sense of purpose has changed everything for me. And I''m grateful to be building it alongside people I respect, trust, and genuinely enjoy doing this with.\n\nFor the next 50 years, I only want to win with others — and that''s the goal of Founders Row, and of every founder we partner with.\n\nBack to seven days a week in the office… I live for this.\n\n— Jamie',
  true,
  NOW()
);

-- Verify the insert
SELECT id, title, quarter, year, published_date, is_published
FROM newsletters
WHERE is_published = true
ORDER BY published_date DESC;
